<Project>

	<!-- =========================================================================
	     REPOSITORY-WIDE SECURITY GATE - vulnerability Class 2, Scan Gate
	     Enforcement. Introduced by the OWASP Top 10 (2021) security audit and
	     remediation of this platform. Findings, evidence and disposition:
	         docs/security/security-audit-report.md
	         docs/security/remediation-log.md
	         docs/security/risk-register.md

	     WHAT IT DOES: turns an ordinary "dotnet restore" and "dotnet build" into
	     the two automated validation gates the audit is measured against -
	     Gate 1 (static analysis) and Gate 2 (dependency / composition analysis).
	     Without it the platform has no reproducible way to prove, or to keep
	     proving, that a vulnerability has not been reintroduced.

	     WHY MSBUILD AND NOT A REPOSITORY-ROOT .editorconfig: all four
	     .editorconfig files in this repository declare "root = true" -
	     WebVella.Erp, WebVella.Erp.Web, WebVella.Erp.Plugins.SDK and
	     WebVella.Erp.Plugins.Next (see WebVella.Erp/.editorconfig line 5). A
	     root .editorconfig would therefore be ignored inside all four subtrees,
	     which is precisely where the core and web libraries live.
	     Directory.Build.props is imported implicitly by MSBuild for every
	     project beneath this directory regardless of .editorconfig scoping, so
	     it reaches all 19 projects in the tree, whether a build is driven from
	     WebVella.ERP3.sln or from an individual project file.

	     THIS FILE IS THE WHOLE BUILD-LEVEL GATE - there is no companion analyzer
	     configuration file, and none may be added. An earlier revision paired
	     this file with a repository-root .globalconfig that promoted ten security
	     rules to errors and bounded the cost of the taint-dataflow family. That
	     file has been removed and the gate returned to the scope the security
	     remediation plan freezes for it: dependency auditing with the audit
	     diagnostics promoted to errors, plus the .NET analyzers at the
	     recommended level. The plan states the escalation position explicitly -
	     "only the dependency diagnostic codes become errors" - so a per-rule
	     analyzer severity block was outside it, and the measured cost of the
	     category upgrade it existed to bound is recorded with the analyzer
	     properties below. The residual coverage this gives up is not implied
	     away: it is enumerated in docs/security/risk-register.md.

	     REPRODUCIBILITY: the SDK is pinned in global.json. Several properties
	     below already match the current SDK defaults; they are stated
	     explicitly anyway so the gate is defined by this file rather than by an
	     SDK default that changes between toolchain versions.

	     DELIBERATELY NOT SET HERE, and not to be added: no target framework,
	     language version, nullable or implicit-usings setting; no package
	     metadata; no package references of any kind; no central package version
	     management; no lock file; and no import of a companion .targets file.
	     Each project owns its own compilation and packaging settings, and
	     centralising them would change how all 19 projects build or publish -
	     out of scope for a security remediation, and recorded instead in
	     docs/security/risk-register.md (finding L-07).
	     ========================================================================= -->

	<!-- Threat addressed: use of components with known vulnerabilities
	     (OWASP A06:2021 - Vulnerable and Outdated Components; CWE-1104,
	     CWE-1035). Serves Validation Gate 2 - "dependency scan: 0 Critical/High
	     CVEs" - by making NuGet audit the advisory database on every restore.

	     Mode "all" rather than "direct" is load-bearing, not belt-and-braces:
	     this graph has carried a transitive-only advisory (MimeKit, reached
	     through MailKit in WebVella.Erp.Plugins.Mail). Under "direct" such an
	     advisory is never reported at all, so the gate would pass while the
	     platform shipped a vulnerable component.

	     Level "low" is the lowest reporting threshold, so a Moderate advisory
	     cannot hide beneath a High-only bar and go unrecorded. -->
	<PropertyGroup>
		<NuGetAudit>true</NuGetAudit>
		<NuGetAuditMode>all</NuGetAuditMode>
		<NuGetAuditLevel>low</NuGetAuditLevel>
	</PropertyGroup>

	<!-- Threat addressed: a dependency advisory that is reported but ignored -
	     an audit that only warns is not a gate, and warnings are routinely
	     scrolled past. NU1901/NU1902/NU1903/NU1904 are the NuGet audit
	     diagnostics for low/moderate/high/critical severity respectively;
	     promoting them makes any advisory in the graph FAIL the build.

	     EXPECTED CONSEQUENCE, BY DESIGN: while any referenced package carries an
	     advisory, restore and build fail. That is the gate working, not a
	     defect. The advisory must be cleared by upgrading the package, or
	     consciously accepted via the narrowly scoped suppression seam at the end
	     of this file together with a recorded risk acceptance - never by
	     deleting this property group.

	     APPENDED, never overwritten: the .NET SDK itself contributes NU1605
	     (package downgrade) and SYSLIB0011 (BinaryFormatter obsoletion, itself a
	     CWE-502 signal) to this property. Assigning a bare literal here would
	     silently drop both.

	     Threat addressed by NU1900 and NU1905 - A GATE THAT FAILS OPEN. The four
	     severity diagnostics above can only fire when the audit actually obtained
	     a vulnerability database. When it did not, the audit reports nothing and
	     restore succeeds, so a package carrying a known High advisory passes
	     every gate in complete silence. Two configurations produce exactly that:
	         - NU1900, "error occurred while getting package vulnerability data" -
	           a package source that is reachable but whose advisory endpoint is
	           not. An internal mirror, an Azure Artifacts upstream, an
	           air-gapped runner or a blocked egress proxy all land here.
	         - NU1905, "audit source provided no vulnerability database" - a
	           NuGetAuditSource is configured but serves no advisory data.
	     THREE configurations were reproduced against this very file, each with a probe
	     referencing a package that carries a published High advisory. They do NOT behave
	     alike, and the differences are recorded here rather than summarised, because
	     overstating what this property group achieves would itself be a fail-open. Each
	     row below is observed output, not expectation; each is reproduced by restoring a
	     single-PackageReference probe under this file with the stated nuget.config:
	         1. Audit endpoint unreachable - auditSources names a host that does not
	           resolve, the shape a dead egress proxy or a blocked mirror takes.
	           Observed: restore exit 1, with BOTH
	           `error NU1900: Warning As Error: Error occurred while getting package
	           vulnerability data` and `error NU1905: Warning As Error: Audit source ...`.
	           CLOSED BY THIS PROPERTY GROUP.
	         2. Audit source reachable but serving no advisory data - auditSources names a
	           local folder, the shape an internal mirror without advisory endpoints takes.
	           Observed: restore exit 1, `error NU1905: Warning As Error: Audit source ...`.
	           CLOSED BY THIS PROPERTY GROUP.
	         3. No audit source resolvable at all - packageSources cleared to a single local
	           folder holding the vulnerable package, no auditSources element. Observed:
	           restore exit 0, the vulnerable package resolved, and ZERO NuGet diagnostics
	           of ANY severity. This is the real fail-open: there is no code to promote, so
	           this property group cannot close it, and saying so is the point of listing it.
	           It is closed instead by the workflow's advisory negative control, which
	           restores a knowingly vulnerable probe and fails the job when that restore
	           SUCCEEDS - the only mechanism that detects an advisory database which is
	           absent rather than merely unreachable.
	     The principle behind all three is the audit's own premise for elevating H-19: a
	     "clean" scan taken from an incomplete graph is worthless, and a clean scan taken
	     from an absent advisory database is worthless for the same reason.

	     DISCLOSED TRADE-OFF, accepted deliberately: a transient nuget.org
	     interruption now fails the build instead of passing it quietly. That is
	     the correct direction for a gate whose entire purpose is to be
	     trustworthy - an unavailable-data build is retried, whereas a
	     falsely-green build is shipped. The failure is also self-describing: the
	     NU1900 text names the source it could not reach. Never demote these two
	     codes to make a flaky network quiet; re-run the job instead.

	     Belt-and-braces, and NECESSARILY so rather than as a nicety: as recorded
	     above, the third of the three fail-open configurations emits no diagnostic
	     for this property group to promote. The workflow's advisory negative control
	     is what covers it, and neither mechanism is redundant with the other.
	     Removing either reopens a configuration in which every gate in this
	     repository reports green on a package with a known High advisory. -->
	<PropertyGroup>
		<WarningsAsErrors>$(WarningsAsErrors);NU1900;NU1901;NU1902;NU1903;NU1904;NU1905</WarningsAsErrors>
	</PropertyGroup>

	<!-- Threat addressed: insecure coding patterns reaching production
	     unreviewed - OWASP A02:2021 (Cryptographic Failures), A03:2021
	     (Injection) and A08:2021 (Software and Data Integrity Failures).
	     Serves Validation Gate 1 - "SAST scan: 0 Critical, 0 High".

	     DISCLOSED SUBSTITUTION: the external SAST tooling named in the audit
	     brief could not be installed in this environment, so the .NET SDK's
	     built-in Roslyn analyzers are the disclosed substitute. This keeps the
	     gate dependency-free - no analyzer package is added, which the audit's
	     "no new package dependency" constraint requires. The substitution is
	     recorded in docs/security/security-audit-report.md.

	     THE GATE IS EXACTLY THESE TWO PROPERTIES, and it is deliberately no
	     wider. The remediation plan freezes the build-level analyzer gate at
	     ".NET analyzers at the recommended level", and it declines analyzer
	     escalation in terms: "only the dependency diagnostic codes become
	     errors". A category-level widening, a per-rule severity promotion, or a
	     global analyzer configuration file to carry either of them all sit
	     outside that agreed scope, so none of them is present here or anywhere
	     else in this repository.

	     "latest-recommended" widens the rule set beyond the SDK default mode.
	     The security families it enforces on its own, each verified by building
	     a probe containing a deliberate violation, are:
	         - weak and broken hashing algorithms   CA5350 / CA5351
	         - disabled certificate validation      CA5359
	         - deprecated transport protocols       CA5364
	     Those four are the build-level SAST substitute, and the workflow's
	     Gate 1 step asserts each is still executing, so a future analysis-level
	     change cannot switch them off while the build stays green.

	     WHAT THIS LEVEL DOES NOT COVER IS RECORDED HERE RATHER THAN IMPLIED
	     AWAY. The remaining security families bearing on this audit's findings
	     ship DISABLED at this level, and an analysis-level change alone does not
	     turn them on - verified by probe, they stayed silent against deliberate
	     violations:
	         - query construction                   CA2100
	         - unrestricted type-name handling      CA2326 / CA2327 / CA2328
	         - ASP.NET Core cookie security         CA5382 / CA5383
	         - hard-coded encryption key            CA5390
	         - non-random initialisation vector     CA5401 / CA5402
	         - disabled token-validation checks     CA5404
	         - interprocedural taint dataflow       CA3001-CA3012
	     Activating them needs a per-rule severity entry of the form
	     dotnet_diagnostic.[rule-id].severity, which needs a global analyzer
	     configuration file to reach past the four "root = true" .editorconfig
	     files; and the taint family needs per-rule cost tuning on top of that
	     merely to terminate - enabled untuned it took the solution build from
	     102 seconds to over 110 minutes without completing, because
	     WebVella.Erp.Web compiles 395 Razor views into a single compilation.
	     Neither that file nor the widening is inside the frozen scope of this
	     gate, so the weaknesses those rules would have flagged were established
	     by MANUAL REVIEW instead: every one of C-01 through C-05 and H-01
	     through H-20 is traceable to a file-and-line locator in
	     docs/security/security-audit-report.md, and the residual tooling
	     coverage gap is carried as an accepted risk in
	     docs/security/risk-register.md rather than left as an unstated
	     assumption. Do not close the gap by re-introducing a global analyzer
	     config here; raise it as a scope change first.

	     PASS CRITERION: no INCREASE over the baseline the workflow's Gate 1 step
	     enumerates for the executing families, deliberately not zero, because
	     roughly 700 pre-existing files are in scope of the analyzers but not of
	     this remediation. Those baselines must not be "fixed" by weakening this
	     file: notably CA5351 on the legacy password-verification path, which
	     exists precisely so already-stored credentials keep working, and CA5351
	     on the general-purpose digest helpers, which are outside the remediated
	     key handling.

	     NO BLANKET WARNINGS-AS-ERRORS SWITCH IS SET, and none may be added.
	     Enabling analyzers across roughly 700 pre-existing source files surfaces
	     a large legacy backlog of general code-quality diagnostics; promoting
	     THAT wholesale would force exactly the repository-wide refactor the
	     remediation scope forbids and would leave the build permanently red.
	     Escalation of a CA rule is therefore not performed at all - it is
	     declined by the remediation scope, not merely deferred. For the same
	     reason no CA identifier is added to the WarningsAsErrors promotion
	     above, and build-time code-style enforcement is left off. The analysis
	     mode is not set separately either: it is already carried by the suffix
	     of AnalysisLevel below, and setting both invites a silent conflict.

	     The analyzer diagnostics stay WARNINGS, which means they do not gate the
	     build on their own. That is intentional and it is why the workflow
	     measures them explicitly: Gate 1 there compares the observed per-rule
	     counts against a recorded baseline and fails on any increase, so a
	     regression in the four executing families is caught even though nothing
	     is promoted to an error here. -->
	<PropertyGroup>
		<EnableNETAnalyzers>true</EnableNETAnalyzers>
		<AnalysisLevel>latest-recommended</AnalysisLevel>
	</PropertyGroup>

	<!-- NO GLOBAL ANALYZER CONFIG IS SUPPLIED, AND NONE MAY BE ADDED. A
	     '.globalconfig' at the repository root is the only mechanism that can
	     deliver a per-rule severity to all 19 projects, because the SDK
	     discovers it by walking every directory above each project rather than
	     by .editorconfig root scoping. That is precisely why it is absent: a
	     per-rule severity promotion is outside the frozen scope of this gate,
	     so the mechanism that would carry one is not present either, and no
	     EditorConfigFiles item is declared here to introduce one by the back
	     door. If a future scope change authorises per-rule escalation, add the
	     file and its measured baselines together with that authorisation - never
	     ahead of it. -->

	<!-- MACHINE-READABLE ANALYZER EVIDENCE - opt-in, and inert unless asked for.
	     Threat addressed: a gate whose verdict cannot be inspected after the
	     fact. Supplying a directory in SecurityAnalyzerSarifDirectory makes each
	     project emit its own SARIF 2.1 log named after itself, so an analyzer
	     verdict can be diffed, filtered or fed to another tool instead of being
	     re-derived by eye from build text. Verified working against this
	     toolchain: one file per compiled project, build exit code unchanged.

	     THIS IS OPERATOR-INVOKED, NOT WIRED INTO CI, and that is deliberate. The
	     security scan workflow publishes only the DERIVED evidence Gate 1
	     extracts - the rule-id list, the diagnostic sets, the allow-list and the
	     unreviewed remainder - because the raw analyzer output runs to megabytes
	     and roughly 3,100 warnings, the overwhelming majority of them the
	     pre-existing backlog the remediation scope declines to refactor.
	     Publishing SARIF for every project would bury the security signal in
	     exactly the noise that policy exists to exclude. So the capability is
	     available on demand and inert by default.

	     Two details are deliberate. The path is PER PROJECT: a single shared
	     ErrorLog file would have the projects overwrite one another and the
	     surviving report would describe only whichever finished last - a gate
	     reporting on a fraction of the solution while appearing complete. And
	     the group is conditional on the property being supplied, so an ordinary
	     local or IDE build writes no SARIF and behaves exactly as before. -->
	<PropertyGroup Condition="'$(SecurityAnalyzerSarifDirectory)' != ''">
		<ErrorLog>$(SecurityAnalyzerSarifDirectory)/$(MSBuildProjectName).sarif,version=2.1</ErrorLog>
	</PropertyGroup>

	<!-- =========================================================================
	     LICENCE GOVERNANCE GATE - review finding CR2-F-04, arising from H-01.
	     Threat addressed: not a code weakness but a supply-chain integrity one.
	     Four manifests in this repository declare
	     <PackageLicenseExpression>Apache-2.0</PackageLicenseExpression> and are
	     published to nuget.org - WebVella.Erp, WebVella.Erp.Web,
	     WebVella.Erp.Plugins.Mail and WebVella.Erp.Plugins.SDK. All four sit on a
	     dependency graph that reaches AutoMapper, which from 15.1.1 onward is
	     published under the Reciprocal Public License 1.5. Publishing an
	     Apache-2.0 claim over that graph states a licence the package does not
	     carry, to every downstream consumer, IRREVOCABLY - a package version
	     cannot be recalled from nuget.org once consumers have resolved it.
	     Only the repository owner may settle that mismatch: changing a product's
	     effective licence posture is a distribution and legal decision, not an
	     engineering one. So rather than either rewriting the expression or
	     leaving a comment and hoping, the unresolved decision is made
	     MECHANICALLY UNSHIPPABLE. See docs/security/risk-register.md (RISK-001).

	     WHY THIS FILE AND NOT THE CORE MANIFEST. An earlier revision placed this
	     target in WebVella.Erp/WebVella.Erp.csproj alone, which left the gate
	     bypassable: `dotnet pack` on WebVella.Erp.Web, .Plugins.Mail or
	     .Plugins.SDK produced a package with the same unratified Apache-2.0
	     expression and no diagnostic at all. That was measured, not suspected -
	     all three packed with exit 0 and the Web package's own .nuspec carries
	     <license type="expression">Apache-2.0</license> beside a dependency on
	     WebVella.Erp, which depends on AutoMapper. Directory.Build.props is
	     imported by every project beneath this directory, so one definition here
	     reaches all four.

	     What it does and does not block, deliberately:
	       - `dotnet pack` FAILS while the decision is unrecorded. Packing is the
	         only action that publishes the licence claim, so it is the only
	         action gated.
	       - `restore`, `build`, `publish`, `run`, `dotnet list package` and every
	         CI gate step are UNAFFECTED. Blocking them would break the security
	         validation this remediation depends on and would penalise developers
	         for a legal question they cannot answer.

	     How the owner clears it - two supported answers, both recorded rather
	     than assumed:
	       - Accept RPL-1.5 (or AutoMapper's commercial agreement) and align the
	         declared expression:
	             dotnet pack -p:ErpAutoMapperLicenceDecision=accepted-rpl-1.5
	       - Decline it: revert the pin per the documented reversal steps in
	         docs/security/risk-register.md (RISK-001), then pack normally.
	         Recording `declined-rpl-1.5` while an RPL-licensed version is still
	         referenced fails on purpose, because the declaration would still be
	         inaccurate.

	     SCOPE, AND WHY IT ERRS TOWARDS OVER-INCLUSION. The gate applies to any
	     project that declares a licence expression, not only to those with a
	     direct reference to the core library. Over-inclusion costs one property
	     on a pack that an owner has to authorise anyway; under-inclusion costs an
	     irrevocable publication. It therefore fails closed in both directions: it
	     also refuses to pack if it cannot read the pin at all (ERPLIC004), so
	     renaming or moving the core manifest disables packaging rather than
	     silently disabling the gate.

	     THE CONFLICT IS DETECTED, NOT HARD-CODED, so the gate disables itself the
	     moment it stops applying: the pinned version is read from the core
	     manifest at pack time and compared against
	     ErpPermissiveAutoMapperVersions below. 14.0.0 is the last MIT release;
	     every release from 15.1.1 onward, which is where the advisory fix begins,
	     is RPL-1.5. If AutoMapper ever publishes a patched permissively licensed
	     release and the pin moves to it, that version is added to the list - a
	     reviewed edit - and packing proceeds with no property required.
	     One practical note: override that list by editing this file rather than
	     from the command line, because MSBuild treats ';' as a command-line
	     property separator and the semicolons would otherwise need escaping
	     as %3B. -->
	<PropertyGroup>
		<ErpCoreProjectFile>$(MSBuildThisFileDirectory)WebVella.Erp/WebVella.Erp.csproj</ErpCoreProjectFile>
		<!-- Every AutoMapper release published under a permissive expression, in
		     both bracketed and bare notation. Extend this list only after reading
		     the release's own .nuspec. -->
		<ErpPermissiveAutoMapperVersions Condition="'$(ErpPermissiveAutoMapperVersions)' == ''">[14.0.0];14.0.0</ErpPermissiveAutoMapperVersions>
	</PropertyGroup>

	<Target Name="ErpAssertAutoMapperLicenceDecisionRecorded"
	        BeforeTargets="GenerateNuspec"
	        Condition="'$(PackageLicenseExpression)' != ''">
		<!-- Read the pin from the single manifest that declares it, so every
		     packable project is judged against the same fact rather than against
		     its own view of the graph. The core manifest has no XML namespace, so
		     a plain XPath applies. -->
		<XmlPeek XmlInputPath="$(ErpCoreProjectFile)"
		         Query="/Project/ItemGroup/PackageReference[@Include='AutoMapper']/@Version"
		         Condition="Exists('$(ErpCoreProjectFile)')">
			<Output TaskParameter="Result" ItemName="ErpAutoMapperPinnedVersionItem" />
		</XmlPeek>
		<PropertyGroup>
			<ErpAutoMapperPinnedVersion>@(ErpAutoMapperPinnedVersionItem)</ErpAutoMapperPinnedVersion>
			<ErpAutoMapperLicenceConflict>false</ErpAutoMapperLicenceConflict>
			<ErpAutoMapperLicenceConflict Condition="'$(ErpAutoMapperPinnedVersion)' != '' AND !$(ErpPermissiveAutoMapperVersions.Contains('$(ErpAutoMapperPinnedVersion)'))">true</ErpAutoMapperLicenceConflict>
		</PropertyGroup>
		<!-- Fail closed when the pin cannot be read, so the gate cannot be
		     disabled by moving or renaming the manifest it reads. -->
		<Error Condition="'$(ErpAutoMapperPinnedVersion)' == ''"
		       Code="ERPLIC004"
		       Text="Packing is blocked: the AutoMapper pin could not be read from '$(ErpCoreProjectFile)', so the licence-governance gate for CR2-F-04 cannot evaluate whether this package's declared PackageLicenseExpression '$(PackageLicenseExpression)' is accurate. This fails closed on purpose - a gate that cannot read its input must not pass. Restore the manifest, or update ErpCoreProjectFile in Directory.Build.props. See docs/security/risk-register.md (RISK-001)." />
		<!-- Fail closed on a malformed answer, so a typo cannot read as consent. -->
		<Error Condition="'$(ErpAutoMapperLicenceDecision)' != '' AND '$(ErpAutoMapperLicenceDecision)' != 'accepted-rpl-1.5' AND '$(ErpAutoMapperLicenceDecision)' != 'declined-rpl-1.5'"
		       Code="ERPLIC003"
		       Text="ErpAutoMapperLicenceDecision is set to '$(ErpAutoMapperLicenceDecision)', which is not a recognised answer. Use 'accepted-rpl-1.5' or 'declined-rpl-1.5'. An unrecognised value is refused rather than ignored, so a typo cannot be mistaken for the owner's consent. See docs/security/risk-register.md (RISK-001)." />
		<Error Condition="'$(ErpAutoMapperLicenceConflict)' == 'true' AND '$(ErpAutoMapperLicenceDecision)' == ''"
		       Code="ERPLIC001"
		       Text="Packing is blocked: $(MSBuildProjectName) declares PackageLicenseExpression '$(PackageLicenseExpression)' while its dependency graph reaches AutoMapper $(ErpAutoMapperPinnedVersion), which is published under the Reciprocal Public License 1.5. Publishing that combination would state a licence this package does not carry, to every downstream consumer, irrevocably. This is a repository-owner decision (RISK-001) and is deliberately not settled automatically. To pack, record the decision: dotnet pack -p:ErpAutoMapperLicenceDecision=accepted-rpl-1.5 - or decline it, revert the pin using the reversal steps in docs/security/risk-register.md (RISK-001), and pack normally. Build, publish and run are unaffected by this gate." />
		<Error Condition="'$(ErpAutoMapperLicenceConflict)' == 'true' AND '$(ErpAutoMapperLicenceDecision)' == 'declined-rpl-1.5'"
		       Code="ERPLIC002"
		       Text="Packing is blocked: the Reciprocal Public License 1.5 is recorded as DECLINED, but AutoMapper $(ErpAutoMapperPinnedVersion) is still referenced, so $(MSBuildProjectName)'s declared licence expression '$(PackageLicenseExpression)' would still be inaccurate. Complete the decline by reverting the pin as documented in docs/security/risk-register.md (RISK-001), which also restores advisory GHSA-rvv3-g6hj-g44x and therefore requires the narrow audit suppression and formal risk acceptance described there." />
		<Message Condition="'$(ErpAutoMapperLicenceConflict)' == 'true' AND '$(ErpAutoMapperLicenceDecision)' == 'accepted-rpl-1.5'"
		         Importance="high"
		         Text="CR2-F-04: packing $(MSBuildProjectName) with the Reciprocal Public License 1.5 recorded as ACCEPTED for AutoMapper $(ErpAutoMapperPinnedVersion). Confirm PackageLicenseExpression '$(PackageLicenseExpression)' still states the intended posture before publishing. See docs/security/risk-register.md (RISK-001)." />
		<Message Condition="'$(ErpAutoMapperLicenceConflict)' != 'true' AND '$(ErpAutoMapperPinnedVersion)' != ''"
		         Importance="high"
		         Text="CR2-F-04: no reciprocal-licence conflict detected for $(MSBuildProjectName) - AutoMapper resolves to a permissively licensed version, so no owner decision is required to pack." />
	</Target>

	<!-- NOTHING IS SUPPRESSED HERE, and no advisory may be suppressed without a
	recorded, justified risk acceptance in docs/security/risk-register.md naming
	the advisory, its severity, why the upgrade is declined and who accepted the
	residual risk. Any such suppression must name ONE narrowly scoped diagnostic
	code - never a blanket NU19* wildcard, and never a wholesale disabling of the
	promotion above - or it defeats Validation Gate 2. -->

</Project>
